sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Since muscular work available scales as the cube of linear size while the rate of energy supply, depending on respiratory surface, scales only as the square, a larger fish should achieve a much higher burst speed than a smaller one but travel a similar distance over a year, and smaller individuals should fatigue faster and have less staying power.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Larger fish can sprint faster but cover similar distances and tire more slowly";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "W_{muscle} \\propto L^3; \\dot{E}_{supply} \\propto S \\propto L^2";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter II, “On Magnitude”; topic “Energy supply and fatigue”. Scope: similar, closely related animals of different size, especially swimming fish"
}
